/-!
# The equal-characteristic structure theorem for complete discrete valuation rings

A complete discrete valuation ring of **equal characteristic** is a power series ring over its
own residue field:
$$ R \;\cong\; \kappa_R[[\pi]] . $$
More precisely, once a uniformizer `π` of `R` is chosen there is a ring isomorphism
`κ_R⟦X⟧ ≃+* R` carrying `X` to `π` and inducing the identity on residue fields
(`Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_irreducible`).

In the equal-characteristic case of Epp's theorem on elimination of wild ramification (Stacks
Tag 09F9), Kuhlmann's corrected argument opens with

> By the structure theorems, `R = 𝔎[[π]]` and `S₀ = 𝔏[[π]]` with `π` a local parameter of `R`
> (a local parameter of `S₀` too, since `S₀ / R` is weakly unramified).

and the Artin–Schreier normal form, the substitution step and Epp's weak-unramifiedness
criterion that follow are all statements about power series; the Stacks Project's own route
fails.

## Equal characteristic, as a hypothesis

The clean form of "equal characteristic" for a local ring `R` is

`(ringChar (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) : R) = 0`,

i.e. the residue characteristic already vanishes in `R`.  It is exactly what separates `𝔽_p[[t]]`
from `ℤ_p`: both have residue field `𝔽_p`, and `(p : 𝔽_p[[t]]) = 0` while `(p : ℤ_p) ≠ 0`.  It
covers characteristic `0` as well, where it holds vacuously.  For a discrete valuation ring it is
implied by `ringChar R ≠ 0` (`Stacks.EppTheorem.natCast_ringChar_residueField_eq_zero`), and it
is stable along extensions of discrete valuation rings
(`Stacks.EppTheorem.ringChar_residueField_eq_of_isExtension`).

## How the isomorphism is built

Three moves, and only the third is specific to discrete valuation rings.

1. **A coefficient field.** The Cohen structure theorem (Tag 032A, available here as
   `IsLocalRing.exists_isCoefficientRing_and_exists_surjective_mvPowerSeries`) provides a
   coefficient ring `Λ ⊆ R`: a complete local subring whose maximal ideal is `Λ ∩ 𝔪` and which
   maps onto `κ_R`.  Its maximal ideal is generated by the residue characteristic, so in equal
   characteristic it is zero and `Λ` is a *field* mapping isomorphically onto `κ_R`.  Inverting
   that isomorphism gives a ring-homomorphism section `σ : κ_R →+* R` of the residue map
   (`Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_residue_comp_eq_id`).

   Note that the Tag 032A provider in this tree yields a surjection from a *multivariate* power
   series ring `MvPowerSeries (Fin n) Λ` — not a map `κ_R⟦X⟧ → R` sending `X` to a uniformizer —
   so part (2) of that theorem is of no use here.  Only part (1), the coefficient ring, is used.

2. **Evaluation at the uniformizer.** `PowerSeries.eval₂Hom` evaluates a power series at a
   topologically nilpotent element of a complete ring.  Every element of `𝔪` is topologically
   nilpotent for the `𝔪`-adic topology, and `IsAdicComplete 𝔪 R` is completeness plus
   separatedness for it (`IsAdic.isAdicComplete_iff`), so `σ` and a uniformizer `π` assemble into
   `F : κ_R⟦X⟧ →+* R` with `F X = π` and `F ∘ C = σ`
   (`Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeries_ringHom`).

3. **`F` is bijective.** Injectivity is formal: `κ_R⟦X⟧` is a discrete valuation ring, so a
   nonzero kernel contains a power of `X`, and `F (X ^ n) = π ^ n ≠ 0`
   (`Stacks.EppTheorem.injective_of_map_irreducible_ne_zero`).  Surjectivity is the successive
   approximation that makes the theorem true: starting from `x`, subtract `σ (residue x)`, divide
   by `π`, and repeat.  The residues collected along the way are the coefficients of a power
   series `f` with `x - F f ∈ 𝔪 ^ n` for every `n`, and Krull's intersection theorem
   (`Ideal.iInf_pow_eq_bot_of_isLocalRing`) forces `x = F f`
   (`Stacks.EppTheorem.surjective_of_residue_comp_eq_id`).

## The weakly unramified pair

Kuhlmann's sentence asks for the two isomorphisms `R ≅ 𝔎[[π]]`, `S₀ ≅ 𝔏[[π]]` to be on a
**common** uniformizer, and that is what
`Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_common_uniformizer` delivers: a uniformizer `π` of
`R` whose image in `S₀` is a uniformizer of `S₀` — this is where weak unramifiedness enters, and
it is all that weak unramifiedness is used for — together with isomorphisms `κ_R⟦X⟧ ≃+* R` and
`κ_{S₀}⟦X⟧ ≃+* S₀` sending `X` to `π` and to `π`'s image respectively.

What is **not** proved here is the stronger compatibility that the square

```
κ_R⟦X⟧  --PowerSeries.map-->  κ_{S₀}⟦X⟧
  |                              |
  ≃                              ≃
  v                              v
  R      --algebraMap-->        S₀
```

commutes.  That needs a coefficient field of `S₀` *containing* the chosen coefficient field of
`R`, i.e. the relative form of step (1) above, which the Tag 032A provider does not supply; the
absolute coefficient fields produced for `R` and for `S₀` are chosen independently.

## Main results

* `Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_residue_comp_eq_id`: in equal characteristic the residue map of a
  complete local ring has a ring-homomorphism section — a coefficient field.
* `Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeries_ringHom`: a coefficient map and an element of an ideal of
  completeness assemble into a map out of a power series ring.
* `Stacks.EppTheorem.injective_of_map_irreducible_ne_zero`,
  `Stacks.EppTheorem.surjective_of_residue_comp_eq_id`: the two halves of bijectivity.
* `Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_irreducible`: the structure theorem with a
  prescribed uniformizer and the residue-field normalisation.
* `Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv`,
  `Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_ringChar`: the structure theorem, with equal
  characteristic stated on the residue characteristic and on `ringChar R`.
* `Stacks.EppTheorem.exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_common_uniformizer`: the pair `R ⊂ S₀` of
  Kuhlmann's first sentence, on a common uniformizer.

## References

* [Epp, *Eliminating wild ramification*, Invent. Math. **19** (1973) 235–249][epp1973],
  Theorem (1.3) and the equal-characteristic case of its proof
* Franz-Viktor Kuhlmann, *A correction to Epp's paper "Elimination of wild ramification"*,
  Invent. Math. **153** (2004) 679–681; [arXiv:1003.5687](https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.5687).
* [The Stacks Project, Tag 09F9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09F9)
* [The Stacks Project, Tag 032A](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/032A), the Cohen structure
  theorem, which supplies the coefficient field
* [The Stacks Project, Tag 0326](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0326), coefficient rings
-/

@[expose] public section

open IsLocalRing

namespace Stacks.EppTheorem

/-! ### Equal characteristic as a condition on the residue characteristic -/

/-- **A discrete valuation ring of positive characteristic is of equal characteristic.**

If `ringChar R ≠ 0` then the residue characteristic of `R` is already zero in `R`.  For a domain
`ringChar R` is prime, and the residue field, being a nonzero quotient, has the same
characteristic; the point of the hypothesis `IsDomain R` is that it rules out `R = ℤ/4`, where
`ringChar R = 4 ≠ 0` while the residue characteristic `2` does not vanish. -/
theorem natCast_ringChar_residueField_eq_zero (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [IsLocalRing R] (h : ringChar R ≠ 0) :
    (ringChar (ResidueField R) : R) = 0 := by
  have : CharP R (ringChar R) := ringChar.charP R
  have : NeZero (ringChar R) := ⟨h⟩
  have hprime : Fact (ringChar R).Prime := CharP.char_is_prime_of_pos R (ringChar R)
  have hcast : ((ringChar R : ℕ) : ResidueField R) = 0 := by
    rw [← map_natCast (residue R), CharP.cast_eq_zero, map_zero]
  have hdvd : ringChar (ResidueField R) ∣ ringChar R := ringChar.dvd hcast
  rcases CharP.char_is_prime_or_zero (ResidueField R) (ringChar (ResidueField R)) with hp | hp
  · rw [(Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq hp hprime.out).mp hdvd, CharP.cast_eq_zero]
  · rw [hp, zero_dvd_iff] at hdvd
    exact absurd hdvd h

/-- **The residue characteristic is unchanged along an extension of discrete valuation rings.**

The induced map `κ_R → κ_S` is a ring homomorphism between fields, hence injective. -/
theorem ringChar_residueField_eq_of_isExtension (R S : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] [CommRing S] [IsDomain S] [IsDiscreteValuationRing S]
    [Algebra R S] [IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsExtension R S] :
    ringChar (ResidueField S) = ringChar (ResidueField R) := by
  have : CharP (ResidueField R) (ringChar (ResidueField R)) := ringChar.charP _
  exact ringChar.eq_iff.mpr (charP_of_injective_ringHom
    (f := ResidueField.map (algebraMap R S)) (RingHom.injective _) (ringChar (ResidueField R)))

/-! ### A coefficient field: the residue map has a ring-homomorphism section -/

/-- **In equal characteristic the residue map of a complete local ring splits.**

The Cohen structure theorem (Tag 032A) provides a coefficient ring `Λ ⊆ R`, whose maximal ideal is
generated by the residue characteristic of `R`.  The hypothesis says that this generator is zero,
so `Λ` is a field, and a coefficient ring maps isomorphically onto the residue field; the inverse
of that isomorphism is the desired section. -/
theorem exists_residue_comp_eq_id (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R]
    (hchar : (ringChar (ResidueField R) : R) = 0) :
    ∃ σ : ResidueField R →+* R, (residue R).comp σ = RingHom.id (ResidueField R) := by
  obtain ⟨Λ, hΛ⟩ :=
    (IsLocalRing.exists_isCoefficientRing_and_exists_surjective_mvPowerSeries R).1
  have := hΛ.isLocalHom
  have hbot : maximalIdeal Λ.toSubring = ⊥ := by
    rw [← IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal_comap Λ.toSubring.subtype,
      hΛ.comap_maximalIdeal_eq_span_ringChar, Ideal.span_singleton_eq_bot]
    exact Subtype.ext (by push_cast; exact hchar)
  let : Field Λ.toSubring := (isField_iff_maximalIdeal_eq.mpr hbot).toField
  have hbij : Function.Bijective ((residue R).comp Λ.toSubring.subtype) := by
    refine ⟨RingHom.injective _, fun y => ?_⟩
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := hΛ.residueField_map_surjective y
    obtain ⟨z, rfl⟩ := residue_surjective x
    exact ⟨z, hx⟩
  exact ⟨Λ.toSubring.subtype.comp (RingEquiv.ofBijective _ hbij).symm.toRingHom,
    RingHom.ext fun x => (RingEquiv.ofBijective _ hbij).apply_symm_apply x⟩

/-! ### Evaluating a power series at an element of an ideal of completeness -/

/-- **A coefficient map and an element of an ideal of completeness assemble into a map out of a
power series ring.**

If `A` is `I`-adically complete and `x ∈ I`, then `x` is topologically nilpotent for the `I`-adic
topology and `A` is complete and separated for it, so `PowerSeries.eval₂Hom` evaluates power
series at `x`.  The coefficient ring `k` carries the discrete topology, for which every map out of
it is continuous. -/
theorem exists_powerSeries_ringHom {k A : Type*} [CommRing k] [CommRing A] (I : Ideal A)
    [IsAdicComplete I A] (f : k →+* A) (x : A) (hx : x ∈ I) :
    ∃ F : PowerSeries k →+* A, F PowerSeries.X = x ∧ F.comp PowerSeries.C = f := by
  let : WithIdeal k := ⟨⊥⟩
  have : DiscreteTopology k := is_bot_adic_iff.mp rfl
  let : WithIdeal A := ⟨I⟩
  obtain ⟨hcs, ht2⟩ := (IsAdic.isAdicComplete_iff (I := I) rfl).mp ‹IsAdicComplete I A›
  let F := PowerSeries.eval₂Hom (φ := f) continuous_of_discreteTopology
    (WithIdeal.isTopologicallyNilpotent_of_mem hx)
  have hF : ⇑F = PowerSeries.eval₂ f x := PowerSeries.coe_eval₂Hom _ _
  refine ⟨F, ?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [hF, PowerSeries.eval₂_X]
  · ext c
    change F (PowerSeries.C c) = f c
    rw [hF, PowerSeries.eval₂_C]

/-! ### Bijectivity of the evaluation map -/

/-- **A ring map out of a discrete valuation ring that does not kill a uniformizer is injective.**

A nonzero ideal of a discrete valuation ring contains a power of any uniformizer, so a nonzero
kernel would make the image of the uniformizer nilpotent. -/
theorem injective_of_map_irreducible_ne_zero {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [IsDomain A]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing A] [CommRing B] [IsReduced B] (F : A →+* B) {π : A}
    (hπ : Irreducible π) (hFπ : F π ≠ 0) :
    Function.Injective F := by
  rw [RingHom.injective_iff_ker_eq_bot]
  by_contra hker
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := IsDiscreteValuationRing.ideal_eq_span_pow_irreducible hker hπ
  have hm : π ^ n ∈ RingHom.ker F := by
    rw [hn]
    exact Ideal.subset_span (Set.mem_singleton _)
  exact hFπ (isNilpotent_iff_eq_zero.mp ⟨n, by simpa using hm⟩)

/-- **Evaluation at a uniformizer is surjective.**

Let `R` be a discrete valuation ring, `σ : κ_R →+* R` a section of the residue map, `π` a
uniformizer, and `F : κ_R⟦X⟧ →+* R` the map with `F X = π` and `F ∘ C = σ`.  Then `F` is
surjective.

This is the successive approximation of Hensel's method, run on coefficients.  Set `w 0 = x`; each
`w n` differs from the lift `σ (residue (w n))` of its own residue by an element of
`𝔪 = (π)`, so `w n - σ (residue (w n)) = π * w (n + 1)` defines the next term.  Writing
`aₙ = residue (w n)`, an induction gives

`x - F (∑_{i < n} aᵢ X ^ i) = π ^ n * w n`,

so the power series `f` with coefficients `aₙ` satisfies `x - F f ∈ 𝔪 ^ n` for every `n` — the
partial sums of `f` are the truncations `PowerSeries.trunc n f`, and `f - trunc n f` is divisible
by `X ^ n`.  Krull's intersection theorem then gives `x = F f`.

Completeness of `R` is not needed: it is used only to *produce* `F`, and once `F` exists the
argument is the separatedness `⋂ₙ 𝔪 ^ n = 0`, which holds in any Noetherian local domain. -/
theorem surjective_of_residue_comp_eq_id {R : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] {σ : ResidueField R →+* R}
    (hσ : (residue R).comp σ = RingHom.id (ResidueField R)) {π : R} (hπ : Irreducible π)
    {F : PowerSeries (ResidueField R) →+* R} (hFX : F PowerSeries.X = π)
    (hFC : F.comp PowerSeries.C = σ) :
    Function.Surjective F := by
  classical
  have hσres : ∀ c : ResidueField R, residue R (σ c) = c := fun c => RingHom.congr_fun hσ c
  have hFCc : ∀ c : ResidueField R, F (PowerSeries.C c) = σ c := fun c => RingHom.congr_fun hFC c
  have hm : maximalIdeal R = Ideal.span {π} := hπ.maximalIdeal_eq
  have hdiv : ∀ y : R, ∃ z : R, y - σ (residue R y) = π * z := by
    intro y
    have hy : y - σ (residue R y) ∈ maximalIdeal R := by
      rw [← residue_eq_zero_iff, map_sub, hσres, sub_self]
    rw [hm, Ideal.mem_span_singleton] at hy
    exact hy
  choose g hg using hdiv
  intro x
  set f : PowerSeries (ResidueField R) := PowerSeries.mk fun n => residue R (g^[n] x) with hf
  refine ⟨f, ?_⟩
  have hcoeff : ∀ n : ℕ, PowerSeries.coeff n f = residue R (g^[n] x) := fun n =>
    PowerSeries.coeff_mk _ _
  have hFmono : ∀ (n : ℕ) (c : ResidueField R),
      F ((Polynomial.monomial n c : Polynomial (ResidueField R)) :
        PowerSeries (ResidueField R)) = σ c * π ^ n := fun n c => by
    rw [← Polynomial.C_mul_X_pow_eq_monomial, Polynomial.coe_mul, Polynomial.coe_C,
      Polynomial.coe_pow, Polynomial.coe_X, map_mul, map_pow, hFX, hFCc]
  have key : ∀ n : ℕ,
      x - F ((PowerSeries.trunc n f : Polynomial (ResidueField R)) :
        PowerSeries (ResidueField R)) = π ^ n * g^[n] x := by
    intro n
    induction n with
    | zero => simp
    | succ n ih =>
      rw [PowerSeries.trunc_succ, Polynomial.coe_add, map_add, hcoeff, hFmono,
        Function.iterate_succ_apply', ← sub_sub, ih, pow_succ]
      linear_combination (π ^ n) * hg (g^[n] x)
  have hmem : ∀ n : ℕ, x - F f ∈ maximalIdeal R ^ n := by
    intro n
    obtain ⟨q, hq⟩ : (PowerSeries.X : PowerSeries (ResidueField R)) ^ n ∣
        f - ((PowerSeries.trunc n f : Polynomial (ResidueField R)) :
          PowerSeries (ResidueField R)) := by
      rw [PowerSeries.X_pow_dvd_iff]
      intro m hmn
      simp [PowerSeries.coeff_coe_trunc_of_lt hmn]
    have hFq : F f - F ((PowerSeries.trunc n f : Polynomial (ResidueField R)) :
        PowerSeries (ResidueField R)) = π ^ n * F q := by
      rw [← map_sub, hq, map_mul, map_pow, hFX]
    rw [hm, Ideal.span_singleton_pow, Ideal.mem_span_singleton]
    refine ⟨g^[n] x - F q, ?_⟩
    linear_combination key n - hFq
  have hzero : x - F f ∈ (⨅ n : ℕ, maximalIdeal R ^ n : Ideal R) :=
    (Submodule.mem_iInf _).mpr hmem
  rw [Ideal.iInf_pow_eq_bot_of_isLocalRing (maximalIdeal R)
    (maximalIdeal.isMaximal R).ne_top] at hzero
  exact (sub_eq_zero.mp (Ideal.mem_bot.mp hzero)).symm

/-! ### The structure theorem -/

/-- **The structure theorem with a prescribed uniformizer: `R ≅ κ_R[[π]]`.**

Let `R` be a complete discrete valuation ring of equal characteristic — the hypothesis `hchar`
says the residue characteristic vanishes in `R` — and let `π` be a uniformizer.  There is a ring
isomorphism `κ_R⟦X⟧ ≃+* R` sending `X` to `π` and inducing the identity on residue fields, in the
sense that the residue of the image of a constant `c` is `c` again.

The isomorphism is pinned down on both the uniformizer and the coefficients, so a power series
identity over `κ_R` transports to an identity in `R` about `π`. -/
@[sga "1 X 2.14" "more general"]
theorem exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_irreducible (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R]
    (hchar : (ringChar (ResidueField R) : R) = 0) {π : R} (hπ : Irreducible π) :
    ∃ e : PowerSeries (ResidueField R) ≃+* R,
      e PowerSeries.X = π ∧ ∀ c : ResidueField R, residue R (e (PowerSeries.C c)) = c := by
  obtain ⟨σ, hσ⟩ := exists_residue_comp_eq_id R hchar
  obtain ⟨F, hFX, hFC⟩ := exists_powerSeries_ringHom (maximalIdeal R) σ π
    ((mem_maximalIdeal π).mpr hπ.not_isUnit)
  have hinj : Function.Injective F :=
    injective_of_map_irreducible_ne_zero F
      ((IsDiscreteValuationRing.irreducible_iff_uniformizer _).mpr
        PowerSeries.maximalIdeal_eq_span_X)
      (hFX ▸ hπ.ne_zero)
  have hsurj : Function.Surjective F := surjective_of_residue_comp_eq_id hσ hπ hFX hFC
  refine ⟨RingEquiv.ofBijective F ⟨hinj, hsurj⟩, hFX, fun c => ?_⟩
  change residue R (F (PowerSeries.C c)) = c
  exact (congrArg (residue R) (RingHom.congr_fun hFC c)).trans (RingHom.congr_fun hσ c)

/-- **The equal-characteristic structure theorem.**

A complete discrete valuation ring of equal characteristic is isomorphic to the power series ring
over its residue field. -/
@[sga "1 X 2.14" "more general"]
theorem exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R]
    (hchar : (ringChar (ResidueField R) : R) = 0) :
    Nonempty (R ≃+* PowerSeries (ResidueField R)) := by
  obtain ⟨π, hπ⟩ := IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_irreducible R
  obtain ⟨e, -, -⟩ := exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_irreducible R hchar hπ
  exact ⟨e.symm⟩

/-- **The equal-characteristic structure theorem, with equal characteristic `ringChar R = p ≠ 0`.**

`ringChar R = p` with `p ≠ 0` is equal characteristic `p`; the hypothesis `p ≠ 0` is what
distinguishes it from the mixed characteristic case, where `ringChar R = 0` and the conclusion is
false (`ℤ_p` is not `𝔽_p⟦X⟧`). -/
@[sga "1 X 2.14" "more general"]
theorem exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_ringChar (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R] {p : ℕ} (hp : p ≠ 0)
    (hchar : ringChar R = p) :
    Nonempty (R ≃+* PowerSeries (ResidueField R)) :=
  exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv R
    (natCast_ringChar_residueField_eq_zero R (hchar ▸ hp))

/-- **The structure theorem for a weakly unramified pair, on a common uniformizer.**

Kuhlmann's opening sentence: for a weakly unramified extension `R ⊂ S₀` of complete discrete
valuation rings of equal characteristic, `R = 𝔎[[π]]` and `S₀ = 𝔏[[π]]` with `π` a local parameter
of `R`, hence — weak unramifiedness being exactly this — of `S₀` too.

Weak unramifiedness is used only to know that the image of a uniformizer of `R` is a uniformizer
of `S₀`: the ramification index is the exponent relating the two uniformizers, so `e = 1` says
they are associates.

The equal-characteristic hypothesis is imposed on `R` alone; it transfers to `S₀` because the
residue characteristic is unchanged along an extension
(`ringChar_residueField_eq_of_isExtension`).

The two isomorphisms are produced independently, and this statement does not claim that the square
formed by them, `PowerSeries.map` and `algebraMap R S₀` commutes; see the module docstring. -/
@[sga "1 X 2.14" "more general"]
theorem exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_common_uniformizer (R S : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R] [CommRing S] [IsDomain S]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing S] [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal S) S] [Algebra R S]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsExtension R S]
    (hwu : IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsWeaklyUnramified R S)
    (hchar : (ringChar (ResidueField R) : R) = 0) :
    ∃ π : R, Irreducible π ∧ Irreducible (algebraMap R S π) ∧
      (∃ e : PowerSeries (ResidueField R) ≃+* R,
        e PowerSeries.X = π ∧ ∀ c : ResidueField R, residue R (e (PowerSeries.C c)) = c) ∧
      (∃ e : PowerSeries (ResidueField S) ≃+* S,
        e PowerSeries.X = algebraMap R S π ∧
          ∀ c : ResidueField S, residue S (e (PowerSeries.C c)) = c) := by
  obtain ⟨π, hπ⟩ := IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_irreducible R
  obtain ⟨πS, hπS⟩ := IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_irreducible S
  obtain ⟨-, -, -, hassoc⟩ := IsDiscreteValuationRing.ramificationIndex_characterization R S
  obtain ⟨u, hu⟩ := hassoc π hπ πS hπS
  rw [hwu, pow_one] at hu
  have hπS' : Irreducible (algebraMap R S π) :=
    (Associated.irreducible ⟨u, by rw [mul_comm]; exact hu.symm⟩ hπS)
  have hcharS : (ringChar (ResidueField S) : S) = 0 := by
    rw [ringChar_residueField_eq_of_isExtension R S, ← map_natCast (algebraMap R S), hchar,
      map_zero]
  exact ⟨π, hπ, hπS', exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_irreducible R hchar hπ,
    exists_powerSeriesRingEquiv_of_irreducible S hcharS hπS'⟩

end Stacks.EppTheorem

end
